<script src="~/lib/jquery/dist/jquery.min.js"></script>
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/toastr.js/2.1.4/toastr.min.js" integrity="sha512-lbwH47l/tPXJYG9AcFNoJaTMhGvYWhVM9YI43CT+uteTRRaiLCui8snIgyAN8XWgNjNhCqlAUdzZptso6OCoFQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer">
</script>
<script>
	toastr.options = {
	  "closeButton": false,
	  "debug": false,
	  "newestOnTop": false,
	  "progressBar": false,
	  "positionClass": "toast-bottom-center",
	  "preventDuplicates": false,
	  "onclick": null,
	  "showDuration": "300",
	  "hideDuration": "1000",
	  "timeOut": "5000",
	  "extendedTimeOut": "1000",
	  "showEasing": "swing",
	  "hideEasing": "linear",
	  "showMethod": "fadeIn",
	  "hideMethod": "fadeOut"
	}
</script>

@if(TempData["ErrorMessage"]!=null)
{
	<script type="text/javascript">
		toastr.error('@TempData["ErrorMessage"]');
	</script>
}

@if(TempData["SuccessMessage"]!=null)
{
	<script type="text/javascript">
		toastr.success(@Json.Serialize(TempData["SuccessMessage"]));
	</script>
}


@* 2️⃣ Why single quotes are necessary

In JavaScript:

toastr.success(Coupon saved successfully); // ❌ INVALID JS


JavaScript must know this is a string, so it must be wrapped in quotes:

toastr.success("Coupon saved successfully"); // ✅
toastr.success('Coupon saved successfully'); // ✅


So the quotes are for JavaScript, not for Razor.

3️⃣ Why SINGLE quotes are commonly used

Because Razor expressions often include double quotes:

@TempData["SuccessMessage"]


If you wrote:

toastr.success("@TempData["SuccessMessage"]"); // ❌ breaks


This causes a syntax error because:

JS string uses "

Razor indexer uses "

So we use single quotes outside to avoid escaping:

toastr.success('@TempData["SuccessMessage"]'); // ✅ clean & readable

4️⃣ What happens if you remove the quotes?
toastr.success(@TempData["SuccessMessage"]); // ❌


After Razor rendering:

toastr.success(Coupon saved successfully);


JavaScript thinks:

Coupon is a variable

saved is another token

➡️ JavaScript runtime error

5️⃣ Important: Razor HTML-encodes output

Razor automatically encodes strings:

TempData["SuccessMessage"] = "Saved <b>successfully</b>";


Output becomes:

toastr.success('Saved &lt;b&gt;successfully&lt;/b&gt;');


This is intentional and safe (prevents XSS).

6️⃣ If message contains apostrophes (edge case)
TempData["SuccessMessage"] = "Coupon user's data saved";


Rendered JS:

toastr.success('Coupon user's data saved'); // ❌ JS error

✅ Safe solution (recommended)

Use JSON encoding:

toastr.success(@Json.Serialize(TempData["SuccessMessage"]));


This outputs:

toastr.success("Coupon user's data saved");


✔ Safe
✔ Correct escaping
✔ Production-ready

7️⃣ Interview-ready explanation

The single quotes are required because the Razor expression outputs a string value into JavaScript, and JavaScript string literals must be quoted. Single quotes are used to avoid conflicts with the double quotes used inside Razor expressions.

🧠 One-line summary

We use single quotes so the Razor-generated value becomes a valid JavaScript string literal. *@